---
import UseCaseLayout from "../../layouts/UseCaseLayout.astro";
import { deployRisk } from "../../data/demo-answers.ts";
import { deactivateFlow } from "../../data/page-answers.ts";
---

<UseCaseLayout
  title="Salesforce Impact Analysis Before You Change or Deploy"
  description="See what breaks before you delete a field, turn off a flow, change a class or deploy. Go, review first or no-go, with every affected component named."
  path="/use-cases/impact-analysis"
  heading="What breaks if I change this?"
  lede="Before you turn off a flow, delete a field, change a class or deploy a release, find out what depends on it. Get a clear verdict and the list behind it, from your org’s real metadata."
  crumb="Impact analysis"
  audience="admins, architects and release managers"
  short="Ask your AI assistant “what breaks if I deactivate this flow?” or “is this deployment risky?”. sf-intelligence traces what depends on each change and answers blocking, review first or go, with every affected component named. Anything it couldn’t fully check is marked “review”, never “safe”."
  answers={[deactivateFlow, deployRisk]}
  answerIntro="Two change questions asked of Verdant, the demo org: turning off one flow, then a small release that deletes a field and changes a flow."
  asks={[
    "What breaks if I deactivate the Project On Approve flow?",
    "Is this deployment risky? Here’s my package.xml.",
    "What happens if I make Permit Approved Date required?",
    "What breaks if I remove the Approved value from Project Status?",
    "What tests should I run for the classes I changed?",
  ]}
  checks={[
    "<strong>Fields, objects, flows, classes and triggers</strong> and everything that depends on them",
    "<strong>A whole release</strong> from a package.xml, a destructiveChanges.xml or a git diff file list",
    "<strong>Overlapping automation</strong>: two things writing the same field, or a save that sets itself off again",
    "<strong>Which Apex tests</strong> exercise the classes you changed",
    "<strong>What-if changes</strong>: required fields, picklist values, field types, method signatures",
  ]}
  limits={[
    "Code that calls things by a name built at run time",
    "Managed packages, integrations and data loads",
    "Kinds of metadata your refresh didn’t pull. A change touching them can’t be called safe",
    "Whether a test that runs your change actually checks it",
  ]}
  tools={["review_change", "get_impact", "what_if_deactivate_flow", "what_if_make_field_required", "what_if_remove_picklist_value", "tests_for_change", "automation_collisions"]}
  related={[
    { href: "/use-cases/what-breaks-if-you-delete-a-field", label: "What breaks if I delete a field?", note: "use case" },
    { href: "/use-cases/salesforce-dependency-analysis", label: "Dependency analysis", note: "trace both directions" },
    { href: "/use-cases/consultants", label: "For consultants", note: "pre-deploy reviews on client orgs" },
    { href: "/blog/salesforce-order-of-execution", label: "Salesforce order of execution, explained", note: "guide" },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: "What can I hand it for a deployment review?",
      a: "A package.xml, a destructiveChanges.xml, or the list of changed files from a git diff. You can also just name the components in your question.",
    },
    {
      q: "When does it say “go”?",
      a: "Only when nothing in the change set is blocked or left unchecked. A component it can’t find, or a kind of metadata it couldn’t check, keeps the answer at “review first” at best.",
    },
    {
      q: "Can it count the records a change would affect?",
      a: "Not from the snapshot, which holds your org’s setup, not its records. With the optional read-only live mode turned on, some answers add record counts, for example how many existing records have a field empty before you make it required.",
    },
  ]}
  cta={{ question: deactivateFlow.question, related: { href: "/demo#demo-deployment-risk", label: "See a full deployment review" } }}
  dateModified="2026-10-10"
>
  <h2>Why “blocking” and not just a list</h2>
  <p>A list of dependents still leaves you to decide what matters. The answer sorts them: <strong>blocking</strong> means something will fail or stop working, <strong>review first</strong> means it couldn’t confirm the change is safe, and <strong>go</strong> means it checked everything the change touches and found nothing. In the first answer above, turning off Project On Approve would stop Permit records being created when a Project is approved, so it’s blocking.</p>
  <h2>Releases, not just single changes</h2>
  <p>New in sf-intelligence 0.4.0, a deployment review takes the change set the way you already have it and returns go, review first or no-go with the reasons. It also checks the objects the release touches for automation that writes the same field twice or sets itself off again.</p>
</UseCaseLayout>
